<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
In nixpkgs, <code>stdenv.mkDerivation</code> and compatible wrappers can derive
<code>name</code> automatically from <code>pname</code> and <code>version</code>
as <code>${pname}-${version}</code>. When a derivation sets
<code>name</code> explicitly alongside <code>version</code> but omits
<code>pname</code>, that convention is bypassed.
</p>
<p>
This is easy to overlook in older expressions that predate widespread
<code>pname</code>/<code>version</code> usage. It also breaks tooling that expects
<code>pname</code> to be present, even if the explicit <code>name</code> happens to
look like a conventional <code>${pname}-${version}</code> string.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Prefer separate <code>pname</code> and <code>version</code> attributes and let the
wrapper derive <code>name</code> automatically. If you intentionally need a custom
<code>name</code>, keep <code>pname</code> as well so downstream tooling can still
identify the package correctly.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  name = "foo";
  version = "1.2";
}
</sample>
<p>Correct:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "foo";
  version = "1.2";
}
</sample>
<p>Also acceptable when a custom derivation name is deliberate:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  name = "foo-minimal";
  pname = "foo";
  version = "1.2";
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/name-and-version.md">nixpkgs-hammering: name-and-version</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
